<div class="<%= layout_framework_classes %>">
  <div class="<%= layout_framework_title_classes %>">
    <%= render 'framework/page_header', page_name: :theme_slots %>
  </div>

  <div class="<%= layout_framework_content_classes %>">
    <%= render_framework_intro_paragraph(:theme_slots) %>

    <div class="space-y-16">
      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Semantic Channels') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Start with the semantic channels: a few lines recolor the whole screen, because every
            component reads them. The set is fixed:
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
            <li>Backgrounds: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">canvas</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">surface</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">backdrop</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fill-strong</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fill-muted</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fill-soft</code></li>
            <li>Text: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-primary</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-secondary</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-inverse</code></li>
            <li>Stroke: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">stroke-contrast</code></li>
            <li>Borders: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-strong</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-muted</code></li>
            <li>Icon: one global channel for <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">image--adaptive</code> icons; it defaults to text-primary. See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :image) %>.</li>
          </ul>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-bg($semantic, $token)</code>: sets a
              background channel to a token.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-text($semantic, $token)</code>: sets a
              text channel to a token, with everything text needs (color, dither pattern, clipping).
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-stroke($semantic, $token)</code>: sets
              the stroke channel to a token.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-border($semantic, $token)</code>: sets a
              border channel to a token's fill.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">semantic-icon($token)</code>: optional color for
              adaptive icons; omit it and icons follow text-primary.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every semantic mixin takes <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$raw: true</code>, which
            writes a plain color and skips device rendering; a theme never needs it.
            See <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :theme_authoring) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '@include theme-slots.semantic-bg("canvas", "yellow");
@include theme-slots.semantic-bg("surface", "yellow");
@include theme-slots.semantic-text("text-primary", "black");
@include theme-slots.semantic-text("text-secondary", "yellow-30");
@include theme-slots.semantic-stroke("stroke-contrast", "yellow");
@include theme-slots.semantic-border("border-muted", "yellow-30");',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Component Slots') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Component slots recolor one surface, like the title bar, without moving a whole channel. Each slot is
            named after the part it colors and takes one kind of paint. These are the slots components read today:
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-1">
            <li>Background slots: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">screen-backdrop</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">title-bar</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">item-meta</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">item-meta-emphasis-2</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">item-meta-emphasis-3</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-track</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-fill</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-fill-emphasis-2</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-fill-emphasis-3</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-dot</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">progress-dot-current</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">table-meta</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">table-meta-device</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">chip</code></li>
            <li>Text slots: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">label-gray</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">chip</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">title-bar</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">title-bar-instance</code></li>
            <li>Border slots: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">table-head-row</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">table-body-row</code>, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">label-underline</code></li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">chip</code> is two slots for one surface: a solid
            box with readable text on it, for markers the framework has no component for. Its defaults point at the
            semantic channels (box from <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">fill-strong</code>,
            text from <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-inverse</code>), so it follows the
            theme on its own. Set both halves yourself when the box needs a specific pairing, the way White and Red
            gives it a white box with red text.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Using the wrong mixin on a slot fails silently. Give a background slot
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-slot</code> and it writes variables no component
            reads; nothing on the screen changes.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg-slot($slot, $token)</code>: a slot's background
              paint.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-slot($slot, $token)</code>: a slot's text
              paint, dither pattern and clipping included.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-level-slot($slot, $level, $dir: h)</code>:
              sets a slot's border to one of the framework border levels.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-token-slot($slot, $token, $dir: h)</code>:
              sets a slot's border to a token's fill instead of a level.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Slot values cascade down from the screen, so you can set them again on a component or a state wrapper.
            On an unthemed screen, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">inverse</code> flips its subtree to the opposite scheme.
            On a themed screen the subtree keeps the theme's slots; the theme itself restates the slots that should flip, as in the example below.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '@include theme-slots.bg-slot("title-bar", "yellow-40");
@include theme-slots.bg-slot("progress-fill", "yellow-55");
@include theme-slots.text-slot("label-gray", "yellow-30");
@include theme-slots.border-token-slot("label-underline", "yellow-30");

.screen--theme-example .inverse {
    @include theme-slots.bg-slot("progress-fill", "yellow-20");
}',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Title-Bar Inks') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The title bar is a paired surface like the chip: a field with inks on it. Its background is the
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">title-bar</code> background slot above; four more
            slots recolor what sits on the field without moving the screen-wide channels. Unset, each falls back to
            exactly what the bar read before: the title follows
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-primary</code>, the instance follows the title
            (then <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-secondary</code> on 2-bit and up), the
            icon follows the icon channel, and halos follow
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">stroke-contrast</code>.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">ink-slot($slot, $token)</code>: a text slot plus
              the solid companion inherited <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">color</code> needs;
              use it for <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">title-bar</code> and
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">title-bar-instance</code>.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">slot-icon($slot, $token)</code>: paint for the
              bar's mask-recolored icon, ahead of the global icon channel.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">slot-stroke($slot, $token)</code>: the halo
              outline around the bar's glyphs and icon. Point it at the bar's own field tone so outlines separate
              ink from fill instead of tracking the canvas.
            </li>
          </ul>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '// A black bar with white title and icon.
@include theme-slots.bg-slot("title-bar", "black");
@include theme-slots.ink-slot("title-bar", "white");
@include theme-slots.ink-slot("title-bar-instance", "gray-60");
@include theme-slots.slot-icon("title-bar", "white");
@include theme-slots.slot-stroke("title-bar", "black");',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Structure and Weight') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Structure moves through factors: unitless multipliers over the unthemed geometry, so 1 is always the
            unthemed screen and every device density stays correct underneath.
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">layout-factors($whitespace, $corners, $title-bar-height, $meters)</code>
            takes any subset. Whitespace rides the content scale, so gaps, pads, and table rows breathe together;
            corners ride every radius, named and component-owned alike (0 squares the screen off); the other two
            size the title bar with its icon, and the progress bars and dots.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">font-weight-shift($shift)</code> moves every vector
            role weight by one signed amount, clamped to renderable weights where it lands. The bitmap bundles
            carry single-weight faces and ignore it. Explicit utilities
            (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text--bold</code>, numeric
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">rounded--*</code> values) stay literal: they are a
            plugin author's stated intent, not a theme default.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-modifiers(...)</code> sets case and tracking per
            text role: <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$label-transform</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$label-tracking</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$value-transform</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$value-tracking</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$title-transform</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$title-tracking</code>, and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$description-tracking</code>. Sizes and line heights
            are deliberately not part of it: those are the device and reader scale axes, and a theme that moved them
            would fight the reader's own text-scale setting.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Tracking takes whole positive pixels, and the mixin refuses anything else. The bitmap bundles are drawn
            on the pixel grid, so a fractional advance from an
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">em</code> value puts their glyphs off-grid, and a
            negative one eats sidebearings that are part of the drawing. Adding a whole pixel between letters is the
            one move that stays on the grid.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '// Airy, square-cornered, slightly bolder, editorial labels.
@include theme-slots.layout-factors($whitespace: 1.35, $corners: 0);
@include theme-slots.font-weight-shift(100);
@include theme-slots.text-modifiers($label-transform: uppercase, $label-tracking: 1px);',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Utility Remaps') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Utility remaps recolor the raw palette utilities
            (<code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg--*</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text--*</code>,
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">text-stroke--*</code>), so markup that says
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">bg--gray-50</code> follows the theme without edits.
          </p>
          <ul class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %> list-disc pl-6 space-y-2">
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-remap-grayscale($to-hue, $side)</code>: the
              bulk remap, moving the whole grayscale ladder to a hue;
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$side</code> controls how.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-remap-border-grayscale($to-hue, $side)</code>:
              the same remap for border lines: their two inks recolor, their patterns keep their shape.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-bg($token, $remap-to)</code> /
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-text($token, $remap-to)</code> /
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-stroke($token, $remap-to)</code>: remap one
              utility token, for exceptions on top of a bulk remap.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-border-token($level, $dir, $token)</code>:
              remap one border level and direction to a token's paint.
            </li>
            <li>
              <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-border-level($level, $dir, $from-level)</code>:
              swap one border level for another level's line art, colors and rendering included. The 2-bit
              borders need this mixin: their four tones are drawn into the line itself, where no token remap can
              reach them. Restating a level onto itself is not a no-op: it also pins the two ink colors the
              level's line draws with.
            </li>
          </ul>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            The <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$side</code> values:
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'dark'</code> (default) presses the grays into the
            hue's dark steps 10 to 40, keeps black as ink, and sends white to the hue.
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'bright'</code> presses them into steps 45 to 75,
            sends black to the hue, and keeps white. <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">'linear'</code>
            maps gray steps to hue steps 1:1.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Strokes are the one exception: whichever token maps to the hue itself keeps a visible stroke, taken
            from the nearest hue step (45 under bright, 40 under dark, 75 under linear), so outlines never
            dissolve into the canvas.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Black and Yellow remaps to the dark side, and White and Red
            remaps to the bright side with one exception, <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-bg("white", "red")</code>,
            so white surfaces turn red while white text stays ink. Dark flips the grayscale token by token with
            the single-token remaps, and uses
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-border-level</code> for the 2-bit
            borders and for the one level that maps to itself.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '// White and Red: bright-side remap with one exception.
@include theme-slots.utility-remap-grayscale("red", $side: "bright");
@include theme-slots.utility-remap-border-grayscale("red", $side: "bright");
@include theme-slots.utility-bg("white", "red");',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'Border Levels and Lines') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Border levels are the seven steps the framework draws borders in: 1 is black, 2 through 6
            step through the grays (gray-15, gray-30, gray-40, gray-50, gray-65), 7 is white, each with an
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">h</code> and a
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">v</code> direction.
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">border-level-slot</code> sets a component's border
            to a level; <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-border-token</code> recolors a
            level itself, and <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">utility-remap-border-grayscale</code>
            recolors the two line inks in bulk. See
            <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :border) %> and <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :divider) %> for how levels
            render.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Every border slot and remap changes two things at once: the CSS line, and what
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.border()</code> and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.divider()</code> hand to JavaScript.
            Bind a slot to a level and JavaScript gets that level's pattern. Bind it to a token and JavaScript
            gets a flat line in the token's stroke color, because only levels have patterns.
          </p>
        </div>
      </section>

      <section class="space-y-8">
        <div class="space-y-4">
          <%= framework_heading(3, 'The Chart Ramp') %>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">chart-series-ramp($tokens, $span: 6)</code> sets
            the colors charts pick their series from: list the tokens in order, strongest contrast against the
            background first, and series 0 gets the first one. Each entry keeps the token's own rendering, so series
            dither or paint solid per device. JavaScript spreads series across the first
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$span</code> entries of the list.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            A short list is safe. The ramp always has 16 entries; the ones past your last token are cleared
            instead of keeping the framework's default grays, and
            <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">$span</code> never reaches past your last token.
          </p>
          <p class="<%= framework_section_description_classes %>">
            Charts read the ramp through <code class="<%= framework_section_code_classes %>">TRMNLPaint.series()</code>;
            see <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :paint_charts) %> and <%= render DocsRef.new(page: :chart) %>.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="mt-4">
          <%= render CodeExampleComponent.new(
            demo_code: '@include theme-slots.chart-series-ramp((
    black, yellow-10, yellow-20, yellow-30, yellow-40, yellow-55, yellow-75, yellow
));',
            language: 'scss'
          ) %>
        </div>
      </section>
    </div>

    <%= render 'framework/page_navigation' %>
  </div>
</div>
